  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1108 AM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nMORE PRECIPITATION CHANCES TODAY BEFORE A DRIER WEEKEND:\n\nA trough will be kicked out over the Desert Southwest and into the \nFour Corners area today. This will introduce scattered shower and \nstorm chances across western Colorado and portions of eastern Utah. \nRecent HiRes guidance depicts the bulk of storm activity to be along \nand south of I-70. Periods of moderate to occasionally heavy \nrainfall can be expected out of the stronger cells. Locations that \nsaw the sun this morning may see a quicker transition into \nconvective mode than those who started the morning with light \nstratiform rainfall. All that being said, some isolated stronger \nstorms are possible as instability remains fairly moderate. With \nthat, some locations could see small hail and gusty outflow winds. \nThis activity will quiet down by the overnight period as flat \nridging moves back into the area. This will indicate a drier \nweekend, with daytime highs warming by a few degrees. \n\nNEXT WEEK'S MOISTURE:\n\nBy Monday, the forecast becomes a bit more challenging. Another \ntrough arrives to the PacNW coast, albeit stronger than the last \none. This will work to push the ridging east of the Rockies and \nbacking the upper flow to the southwest over the Great Basin. \nMoisture from a pair of tropical systems, including Polo, will be \ndrawn into the southwest flow aloft ahead of the trough. PWATs will \nagain return to upwards of 200% of normal, particularly in southwest \nColorado and southeast Utah. This indicates the start of another wet \nperiod through at least midweek. It is important to note that some \ndiscrepancies exist between models, with the GFS consistently \nshowing a drier solution, whereas the Euro continues to be more \nbullish with the moisture. Additionally, both the GFS and Euro have \ndifferent placements when it comes to the location of the low \npressure system. Cluster solutions also continue to be inconsistent \non how to handle the moisture in addition to the upstream trough, so \nconfidence remains low on a final solution. Bottom line is that the \nstart of next week looks to be wetter, but whether this will be a \nsignificant/anomalous event for the Four Corners area is yet to be \ndetermined. Stay tuned for how this forecast begins to develop.\n\nIn addition to this moisture, daytime highs will also drop by a few \ndegrees come Monday, which also means overnight lows will do the \nsame. If you're catching what I'm putting down, that would indicate \nsnowflakes returning to the forecast for some our highest peaks and \npasses. At this point, these chances look most confined to the San \nJuans and central Divide mountains.",
